WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.177 --> 00:00:05.283
- We move around sites much
like a photographer would

00:00:05.283 --> 00:00:07.506
taking pictures from many locations.

00:00:07.506 --> 00:00:08.665
But with a photographer,

00:00:08.665 --> 00:00:09.855
you have a single frame.

00:00:09.855 --> 00:00:10.979
(machine whirring)

00:00:10.979 --> 00:00:12.491
By using our technology

00:00:12.491 --> 00:00:15.294
now each of those pixels
actually has a 3-D location,

00:00:15.294 --> 00:00:17.293
x,y,z coordinates.

00:00:17.293 --> 00:00:20.122
And we have now brought
that photograph to life

00:00:20.122 --> 00:00:21.789
in three dimensions.

00:00:25.451 --> 00:00:26.981
(upbeat music)

00:00:26.981 --> 00:00:28.459
My name is Scott Lee,

00:00:28.459 --> 00:00:31.191
and I'm the Director of
Operations for CyArk.

00:00:31.191 --> 00:00:34.107
We have to move the
scanner all around the site

00:00:34.107 --> 00:00:36.297
in order to capture every nook and cranny.

00:00:36.297 --> 00:00:39.392
CyArk's mission is to
accurately capture, archive,

00:00:39.392 --> 00:00:41.735
and share the world's cultural heritage.

00:00:41.735 --> 00:00:43.553
What makes the data really unique

00:00:43.553 --> 00:00:46.342
is you can see we can look
at it from every angle.

00:00:46.342 --> 00:00:49.410
So, we can just pop
literally go through a window

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.378
and let's check out the
interior of the chapel.

00:00:51.378 --> 00:00:53.793
We're documenting the physical evidence

00:00:53.793 --> 00:00:55.476
of the people came before us and

00:00:55.476 --> 00:00:59.643
we're documenting for the
people that come after us.

00:01:01.074 --> 00:01:03.915
Sites in general face
numerous threats today

00:01:03.915 --> 00:01:05.456
whether it's environmental

00:01:05.456 --> 00:01:08.187
or the intentional
destruction of heritage,

00:01:08.187 --> 00:01:10.687
especially in the Middle East.

00:01:12.395 --> 00:01:13.605
These sites are being destroyed

00:01:13.605 --> 00:01:16.927
faster than they can be physically preserved.

00:01:16.927 --> 00:01:19.528
Part of this initiative is
to digitally document them,

00:01:19.528 --> 00:01:22.195
so we have a record for humanity

00:01:23.075 --> 00:01:26.886
and possible reconstruction in the future.

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:30.752
CyArk has done over 200
projects on all seven continents

00:01:30.752 --> 00:01:32.620
from Mt. Rushmore, in the United States,

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:35.446
to Chichen Itza in Mexico,

00:01:35.446 --> 00:01:38.371
to the Tower of London in Europe.

00:01:38.371 --> 00:01:40.817
Even the Sydney Opera House in Australia.

00:01:40.817 --> 00:01:42.564
And as we move around these sites,

00:01:42.564 --> 00:01:45.744
we capture billions
and billions of points.

00:01:45.744 --> 00:01:48.744
And we collect, you know, tons of data.

00:01:48.744 --> 00:01:51.084
(intense music)

00:01:51.084 --> 00:01:54.168
This is the data set we
captured in Mexico City.

00:01:54.168 --> 00:01:55.773
This is Templo Mayor.

00:01:55.773 --> 00:01:58.523
What makes this site really unique is

00:01:58.523 --> 00:02:00.794
it was recently discovered
actually in the '70s,

00:02:00.794 --> 00:02:03.521
which for archeology is very recent.

00:02:03.521 --> 00:02:06.963
If you can't come to
Templo Mayor in Mexico City,

00:02:06.963 --> 00:02:10.614
well, pop on your computer and
you can virtually access this.

00:02:10.614 --> 00:02:13.553
We are walking on the
archeology; it’s very fragile.

00:02:13.553 --> 00:02:15.300
You need to be careful where you step,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:16.358
where you put your machines,

00:02:16.358 --> 00:02:17.888
how you document.

00:02:17.888 --> 00:02:19.859
Documenting these sites and storing them

00:02:19.859 --> 00:02:23.859
for future generations
is an important endeavor.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:27.479
I come to work everyday knowing I'm

00:02:27.479 --> 00:02:29.309
doing something good for humanity.

00:02:29.309 --> 00:02:31.180
Because without knowing our past,

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.263
we won't know our future.

